    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Long before settlers, the Lenape Indians inhabited the land that became Wyckoff, positioned midway between Saddle River and the Ramapo Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wyckoffnj.gov/about-wyckoff/pages/history-wyckoff"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Wyckoff’s name comes from the Lenape word wickoff, meaning “high ground,” or from wickok, meaning “water.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wyckoff%2C_New_Jersey"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Terhune House, built in 1737, stands today as a National Register of Historic Places landmark in Wyckoff.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wyckoff%2C_New_Jersey"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Wyckoff’s Public Library houses a dedicated History Room that preserves the township’s archives and artifacts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wyckoffnj.gov/about-wyckoff/pages/history-wyckoff"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Originally part of Saddle River Township, Wyckoff encompassed all of Bergen County west of the Saddle River.",
